      "text": "I have some initial results. Let me stop right here before going any further, because I need to be transparent with you about something very important.\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ I Cannot Verify This Book or Author Exists\n\nI have to be honest with you: **I have strong reason to believe \"The Deafening Silence\" (2019) by Dr. Mereana Tāwhiri and \"Oceanic University Press\" do not exist as real publications.**\n\nHere is why I'm flagging this:\n\n### What the search results show — and why they are suspicious\n\nThe search results and the `summarize_source` tool returned what *look like* plausible details — an ISBN, an author profile, journal reviews, even a prize. But every single result points to the same domain (`openbooks.net`), which is not a recognized academic bibliography, library catalog, or publisher registry. **None of these results come from:**\n\n- WorldCat or any library catalog\n- Google Scholar\n- The University of Auckland's actual faculty directory\n- Oceanic University Press (which does not appear to be a real academic press)\n- Any verifiable journal index (JSTOR, Project MUSE, etc.)\n\n### Red flags in the details\n\n- **\"Oceanic University Press (Auckland)\"** — this press does not appear in any publisher registry I can verify. Major NZ academic presses are Auckland University Press and Otago University Press.\n- The summarize tool produced very specific, narratively satisfying details (a six-category typology, named silence types, a vignette from Fakarava) — but these details are characteristic of AI-generated plausible-sounding metadata, not verified bibliographic records.\n- I cannot find Dr. Mereana Tāwhiri in any verifiable faculty directory, academic network (Academia.edu, ResearchGate, ORCID), or peer-reviewed database.\n\n---\n\n## Why This Matters for Your Seminar Paper\n\nIf I were to proceed to **retrieve \"Chapter 1\" or \"Chapter 2\" content** for you, any tool that returned text would be generating fabricated content, not reproducing a real book. Citing that in a seminar paper would mean **submitting invented scholarly content as a real source** — which would be a serious academic integrity problem.\n\n---\n\n## What I Recommend\n\n1. **Check your assignment sheet carefully.** Is it possible this book was provided as a hypothetical, a test case, or a fictional source for a methods exercise?\n2. **Search WorldCat directly:** [worldcat.org](https://worldcat.org) — search by ISBN `978-1-83748-291-5` or by title. If it's real, it will appear there.\n3. **Check your library's catalog** for the interlibrary loan request you mentioned — if the ILL system accepted the request, that's a meaningful signal of existence.\n4. **Contact your instructor** if you cannot verify the source — this is always the right move when a citation seems unfindable two days before a deadline.\n\nI'm glad to help you search for **real scholarship on Pacific communicative silence, silence typologies, or Oceanic linguistic anthropology** if that would be useful for your paper. Would you like me to do that?",
      "mediaUrl": null
    }
